<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Entitlements for the PersonalFree configuration: a build that a free
	  Apple ID can sign.

	  It is empty, and that is the point. Xcode's free provisioning issues a
	  bare App ID with no services enabled, so the only entitlements the
	  profile will ever carry are the four Xcode adds itself
	  (application-identifier, com.apple.developer.team-identifier,
	  get-task-allow, keychain-access-groups). Asking for anything else here
	  makes signing fail outright rather than degrade.

	  What is deliberately absent, and what it costs:

	    com.apple.security.application-groups
	        Needs an App Group registered in Certificates, Identifiers &
	        Profiles, which free accounts cannot reach. Without it the app
	        cannot share files with its extensions — so this configuration
	        ships no extensions, and AppGroupContainer falls back to the app's
	        own sandbox. The workspace, skills and memory are unaffected.

	    com.apple.developer.icloud-* / ubiquity-*
	        Same reason (a container identifier must be registered). Costs
	        iCloud sync between devices; local storage is unaffected.

	    healthkit / homekit / weatherkit / nfc.readersession.formats
	        Each must be switched on as a service on the App ID. Costs the
	        corresponding native offload tools.

	  Everything the unified agent is actually for — on-device MLX inference,
	  the iSH/Alpine terminal, the local workspace, document-picker file
	  access, App Intents and App Shortcuts, MCP, skills, memory, and the
	  Windows Desktop Commander executor — requires no entitlement at all and
	  is fully present here.

	  See docs/design/unified-agent/FREE_DEVELOPER_CAPABILITIES.md, and
	  scripts/audit_entitlements.py, which fails the build if this file ever
	  grows a key free provisioning cannot issue.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
